NAME
    sleep - pause for a number of seconds

SYNOPSIS
    sleep SECONDS

DESCRIPTION
    Sleeps for SECONDS (integer, 0-600). Implemented as a loop of
    100 ms sys_sleep_ms calls so that SIGKILL can interrupt within
    one chunk — pit_sleep itself doesn't check pending signals,
    but each chunk's iret-to-ring-3 boundary does.

    SECONDS is capped at 600 (10 minutes) for selftest hygiene —
    no realistic interactive use needs more than that, and a runaway
    sleep can hold task table slots otherwise.

EXAMPLES
    sleep 1
    sleep 10                            # 10-second pause

EXIT STATUS
    0   slept for the full duration
    1   bad argument (non-numeric, negative)

SEE ALSO
    kill(1), ps(1)

NOTES
    If you SIGKILL a running sleep, the kill lands within ~100 ms
    of the cancel — the chunked design intentionally bounds the
    delivery latency.
